How long does it take for a cup of coffee to cool down? This project studies the cooling of coffee at home using Newton's law of cooling, which states that the rate at which a hot body loses heat is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings.

We measured the temperature of two cups of coffee, one with a lid and one without, every two minutes for forty minutes, using a digital thermometer and a stopwatch. The data were fitted to an exponential model by linear regression of the logarithm of the temperature difference.

Both cups follow Newton's law very closely ($R^2 > 0.999$). The cooling constant of the uncovered cup ($k = 0.045 thin "min"^(-1)$) is about 1.6 times larger than that of the covered cup ($k = 0.028 thin "min"^(-1)$), so a lid keeps coffee above 60 °C for seven extra minutes. These results show that evaporation from the open surface plays an important role in everyday cooling.

*Keywords:* Newton's law of cooling, heat transfer, exponential decay, linear regression.
